WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:15.699
BEN: Hey there, Little Fox readers! This week on People in the News , Olivia is going to

2
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:23.793
tell you about Bill Gates, the co-founder of Microsoft, a very successful computer software company. Bill is also

3
00:00:23.794 --> 00:00:29.197
one of the wealthiest people in the world and a passionate philanthropist—someone who believes in using his money

4
00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:38.198
to help others. OLIVIA: Bill was born on October 28, 1955, in Seattle, Washington. His father was a

5
00:00:38.199 --> 00:00:43.442
lawyer, and his mother worked with United Way, an organization aimed at improving education and fighting poverty. Bill

6
00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:49.862
was especially close to her, and she influenced him quite a bit. As a child Bill displayed many

7
00:00:49.863 --> 00:00:55.678
traits that would benefit him later, when he entered the business world. He was very competitive in sports

8
00:00:55.679 --> 00:01:00.850
and board games, and he loved to read. He particularly enjoyed reading reference books like the encyclopedia. Despite

9
00:01:00.851 --> 00:01:08.658
his good grades in school, Bill's parents became worried because he often seemed quiet and withdrawn. They wondered

10
00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:14.394
if he was bored by school and needed a more challenging environment. When Bill was 13, he transferred

11
00:01:14.395 --> 00:01:24.413
to the Lakeside School, a prestigious school in Seattle where students had to work hard to get good

12
00:01:24.414 --> 00:01:29.324
grades. He did very well there, especially in math and science. At Lakeside School, Bill also discovered something

13
00:01:29.325 --> 00:01:36.728
that would shape the direction of his life: a computer lab. The year was 1968, and most schools

14
00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:46.294
and businesses didn't own computers —the personal computer hadn't even been invented yet. Bill was mesmerized by the

15
00:01:46.295 --> 00:01:55.040
computer, and he spent hours in the lab, experimenting and learning how to write computer programs. Bill was

16
00:01:55.041 --> 00:01:59.633
only 15 when he made his first computer-related sale. He and his friend Paul Allen had written a

17
00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:09.285
computer program called "Traf-O-Data," which kept track of traffic patterns in the city of Seattle. The city purchased

18
00:02:09.286 --> 00:02:18.701
this program from the boys, paying $20,000 for it. Not bad for two teenagers! After Bill graduated high

19
00:02:18.702 --> 00:02:23.706
school, he enrolled at Harvard University. One day he and Paul read about a new personal computer called

20
00:02:23.707 --> 00:02:29.769
the Altair. They contacted the company that made the computer and offered to write software for it. The

21
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:35.338
company accepted their proposal, and the two friends spent a couple of months developing the program. BEN: That's

22
00:02:35.339 --> 00:02:41.900
pretty impressive. But did their software actually work? OLIVIA: Yep, it worked perfectly. And soon after the project

23
00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:46.668
was completed, Bill dropped out of Harvard, and he and Paul formed their own company, Microsoft. In addition

24
00:02:46.669 --> 00:02:58.322
to being a brilliant computer programmer, Bill proved to be a very shrewd businessman. In 1980 the company

25
00:02:58.323 --> 00:03:04.798
IBM was developing a new personal computer, and they hired Microsoft to write the operating system for it.

26
00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:14.729
Bill and Paul called the software MS-DOS. Rather than sell MS-DOS only to IBM, Bill had the smart

27
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:26.646
idea to charge the company a licensing fee for it. This way he could license the same software

28
00:03:26.647 --> 00:03:34.130
to other computer manufacturers—and make even more money! Within two years, thirty percent of all the computers in

29
00:03:34.131 --> 00:03:41.123
the world were running on MS-DOS. By 1986 Bill was a millionaire; a year later he was a

30
00:03:41.124 --> 00:03:47.375
billionaire . For many years Bill has been ranked as the wealthiest person in the world. His wealth

31
00:03:47.376 --> 00:03:52.016
peaked in 1999, when it reached $101 billion. Can you imagine having that much money, Ben? BEN: Yes,

32
00:03:52.017 --> 00:03:59.013
Olivia, and right now I'm imagining everything I would buy! OLIVIA: Well, luckily for the world, Bill was

33
00:03:59.014 --> 00:04:02.113
influenced by his mother's generosity, and he turned to philanthropy. In 1999 he and his wife formed the

34
00:04:02.114 --> 00:04:06.864
Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, which focuses on issues related to health, poverty, and education. The foundation provides

35
00:04:06.865 --> 00:04:15.189
money to scientists and doctors working to fight malaria and other diseases that affect people in poor countries.

36
00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:25.764
Bill and Melinda also fund efforts to eradicate hunger and improve living conditions around the world. For instance,

37
00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:34.338
they work with researchers developing crops that are resistant to droughts or floods, and they're exploring ways to

38
00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:41.408
improve sanitation and water supplies in poor villages. They're also working in the U.S. to ensure that everyone

39
00:04:41.409 --> 00:04:52.824
gets a high-quality education. Being a computer guy, Bill is committed to making sure that every library in

40
00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:58.401
the U.S. has computers and high-speed Internet access. Bill doesn't just write checks for these things; he is

41
00:04:58.402 --> 00:05:07.459
very involved in the whole process. He has researched how to be effective with philanthropy, and he makes

42
00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:13.712
sure the foundation's money is being spent wisely. A few times a year, Bill and Melinda travel to

43
00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:21.378
some of the world's poorest places so they can see firsthand just how awful poverty is and measure

44
00:05:21.379 --> 00:05:33.447
their foundation's progress. I don't know about you, Ben, but I find it amazing that a man who

45
00:05:33.448 --> 00:05:42.934
can afford to stay at any hotel in the world actually sleeps in a mud hut once in

46
00:05:42.935 --> 00:05:50.863
a while! Bill became so passionate about his philanthropy that he decided to stop working at Microsoft in

47
00:05:50.864 --> 00:05:55.002
2008. Since then he has worked full-time at the foundation. In 2010 Bill and Melinda, along with billionaire

48
00:05:55.003 --> 00:06:04.083
Warren Buffett, began asking other billionaires to sign the Giving Pledge. Those who sign it promise to give

49
00:06:04.084 --> 00:06:11.829
away at least half of their wealth, either during their lifetimes or when they die. And that's what

50
00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:19.257
Bill plans to do. Almost all of his fortune will eventually go toward helping people around the world.

51
00:06:19.258 --> 00:06:26.472
In fact, he's no longer the richest person in the world simply because he's already given so much

52
00:06:26.473 --> 00:06:34.313
away! BEN: Now that's what I call generous. If I had billions of dollars, I'd sign the Giving

53
00:06:34.314 --> 00:06:42.649
Pledge too. We'll see you soon, Little Fox readers, with another edition of People in the News .

